# Why do governments fail when politicians follow their incentives?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What did Buchanan's public choice theory assume about politicians and voters? Why is voter ignorance rational?"
next: {"title":"Who captures the regulator?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/57-who-captures-the-regulator.cast"}

## Politics without romance
sources:
    - id: kff2025
      title: 'KFF Health Tracking Poll February 2025: The Public''s Views on Global Health and USAID'
      authors: KFF
      year: 2025
      finding: On average US adults guessed foreign aid is 26% of the federal budget; about 1% is; 11% estimated about 1% or less (1,322 adults, 18–25 Feb 2025).
      url: https://www.kff.org/global-health-policy/poll-finding/kff-health-tracking-poll-february-2025-the-publics-views-on-global-health-and-usaid/
    - id: nobel1986
      title: The Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel 1986
      authors: Nobel Foundation
      year: 1986
      finding: Awarded to James M. Buchanan Jr. for his development of the contractual and constitutional bases for the theory of economic and political decision-making.
    - id: buchanan1962
      title: The Calculus of Consent
      authors: James M. Buchanan, Gordon Tullock
      year: 1962
      finding: 'Founding text of public choice: political decisions analysed with the same self-interested individuals as markets.'

    icon capitol size 130 set twemoji x 500 y 560 or ["bank","office building"] icon_look picture of "classical building" hidden true
    scale aid cites ["kff2025"] x 100 y 330 width 800 font_size 34 label "Foreign aid, % of US federal budget" unit % min 0 max 50 value 1 ticks 5 markers [{"value":26,"label":"average guess: 26%"}] hidden true
    text cue_right "about right: 1 in 10" cites ["kff2025"] x 500 y 200 font_size 32 hidden true
    portrait buchanan x 850 y 470 width 190 of "James M. Buchanan" cameo true hidden true
    text cue_nobel "public choice · Nobel 1986" cites ["nobel1986","buchanan1962"] x 400 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    text cue_same "same people as in the market" x 500 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    icon voter size 160 set twemoji x 200 y 430 or ["person"] icon_look picture of woman hidden true
    text voter_cue "voter:\nown life" x 200 y 275 font_size 36 hidden true
    icon pol size 160 set twemoji x 500 y 430 or ["man in suit","microphone"] icon_look picture of "speaking head" hidden true
    text pol_cue "politician:\nre-election" x 500 y 275 font_size 36 hidden true
    icon off size 160 set twemoji x 800 y 430 or ["man office worker","briefcase"] icon_look picture of "office worker" hidden true
    text off_cue "official:\nbudget, career" x 800 y 275 font_size 36 hidden true
    text cue_romance "politics without romance" #b5482e x 500 y 170 font_size 42 hidden true
    text cue_know "they could know —\nbut do they want to?" #2f6b8f x 650 y 610 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow arrow_know from.x 780 from.y 570 to.x 790 to.y 525 hidden true

    card title "Politics without romance"

Why do governments choose policies that leave most of us worse off, even when the people in charge know exactly what they cost?
    draw capitol

    pause 0.3

B: Start with something voters are asked to judge every election: the budget. In America, what share of federal spending goes to foreign aid?
    draw aid

    ask "Click on the line: what share of US federal spending goes to foreign aid?" on aid store g default "15" tolerance 0.04 voice b
        right: Close: you said {g}; it's about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it's about {g.true}.

About one percent.
    highlight aid

B: In a KFF poll in February 2025, the average gue (@draw aid_marker_1@)ss was twenty-six percent. Only one person (@draw cue_right@) in ten got it roughly right.

So the easy answer writes itself: voters are badly informed. Teach them, and politics gets fixed.
    highlight aid_marker_1 effect circle

    erase aid aid_marker_1 cue_right capitol

B: James Buchanan would say they aren't fools. Not knowing is a sensible choice.
    draw buchanan

Buchanan, with Gordon Tullock, built public choice theory: eco (@draw cue_nobel@)nomics turned on politics. It won him the Nobel prize in 1986.

    pause 0.3

    erase cue_nobel

B: His starting (@draw cue_same@) point was one assumption. The person in the voting booth is t (@draw voter voter_cue@)he same person in the shop, pursuing her own aims.

    erase buchanan

And the  (@draw pol pol_cue@)politician wants to win the next election; (@draw off off_cue@) the official, a bigger budget and a safer career. Not villains, just people. He called it  (@draw cue_romance@)politics without romance.

    pause 0.3

    erase cue_same

So assume ever (@draw cue_know arrow_know@)y official knows the true costs. The question is no longer what the state can know,  (@highlight off@)but what the people running it want to do.

    pause 0.5

## A penny's worth of knowing
vars:
    B:
      value: 5000
      min: 1000
      max: 1000000
      step: 1000
    'N':
      value: 60000000
      min: 10000000
      max: 60000000
    EB:
      expr: B/N
    Nm:
      expr: N/1000000
sources:
    - id: gse2012
      title: What is the probability your vote will make a difference?
      authors: Andrew Gelman, Nate Silver, Aaron Edlin
      year: 2012
      finding: 'In the 2008 US presidential election the average voter had about a 1 in 60 million chance of being decisive; about 1 in 10 million in the closest states (New Mexico, Virginia, New Hampshire, Colorado). Economic Inquiry 50(2): 321–326.'
    - id: downs1957
      title: An Economic Theory of Democracy
      authors: Anthony Downs
      year: 1957
      finding: Because one vote almost never decides an election, it is rational for most voters to stay poorly informed about politics.

    icon voter size 110 set twemoji x 100 y 620 or ["person"] icon_look picture of woman hidden true
    path base #3d3833 points [[90,225],[640,225]] hidden true
    shape cost_bar #b5482e shape rect x 230 y 375 width 180 height 300 style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.8 hidden true
    text cost_val "$200" #b5482e x 230 y 560 font_size 48 hidden true
    text cost_lab "cost of knowing" x 230 y 192 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape ben_bar #2f6b8f shape rect x 520 y 226 width 180 height 3 bind.height "max(3, 1.5*EB)" bind.y "225 + max(3, 1.5*EB)/2" style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text ben_val "${EB:5}" #2f6b8f x 520 y 285 font_size 48 bind {"style.opacity":"min(1, max(0, (0.001-EB)*100000))"} style.opacity 1 hidden true
    text ben_val4 "${EB:4}" #2f6b8f x 520 y 285 font_size 48 bind {"style.opacity":"min(1, max(0, (EB-0.001)*100000)) * min(1, max(0, (0.05-EB)*100000))"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    text ben_val2 "${EB:2}" #2f6b8f x 520 y 285 font_size 48 bind {"style.opacity":"min(1, max(0, (EB-0.05)*100000))"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    text ben_lab "expected benefit" x 520 y 192 font_size 34 hidden true
    text p_read "p = 1 in {Nm:0} million" cites ["gse2012"] x 810 y 420 font_size 38 hidden true
    text b_read "B = ${B:0,}" x 810 y 355 font_size 38 hidden true
    text cue_ri "rational ignorance\nDowns, 1957" #b5482e cites ["downs1957"] x 835 y 270 font_size 38 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch sc work [{"tex":"\\text{benefit} = p \\times B"},{"tex":"\\$5{,}000 \\div 60{,}000{,}000 \\approx \\$0.00008"},{"tex":"\\text{learn if } p \\times B > \\$200"}] x 500 y 430 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "A penny's worth of knowing"

Buchanan's voter is pursuing her own aims. So what is knowing worth to her?
    draw voter base

B: Knowing costs time. Say a few evenings reading up on the budget and the can (@draw cost_bar cost_val cost_lab@)didates: call it two hundred dollars of your time.

And the payoff? A bet (@draw ben_lab@)ter-informed vote changes the outcome only if yours is the vote that breaks a tie.

B: Andrew Gelman, Nate Silver and Aaron Edlin worked out those odds for the 2008 US presidential el (@draw p_read@)ection: on average, one in sixty million.

Now say the better result is worth five tho (@draw b_read@)usand dollars to you, a lot. Multiply it by one in sixty million.

B: Less t (@draw ben_bar ben_val ben_val4 ben_val2@)han a hundredth of a cent. Against two hundred dollars.

    pause 0.5

That's rational ignorance (@draw cue_ri@), Anthony Downs's idea from 1957: when your vote almost never decides, studying for it doesn't pay.

Try it: drag what the outcome is worth to you all the way up to a million dollars.
    explore params ["B"]

B: Still under two cents.
    animate B 1000000 duration 1.5

B: Even in the closest states, about one in ten million, a million-dollar stake buys ten cents.
    animate N 10000000 duration 2

    fade voter base cost_bar cost_val cost_lab ben_bar ben_val ben_val4 ben_val2 ben_lab p_read b_read cue_ri to 0.15

B: Want to see the sum?
    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

    animate B 5000 N 60000000 duration 1

The expected benefit is the chance your vote decides, p, times what the outcome is worth to you, B.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

Five thousand dollars over sixty million is about eight thousandths of a cent.
    draw sc_line_2

Learning pays only if p times B beats the cost, two hundred dollars. It never comes close.
    draw sc_line_3

    erase sc

@test

    erase mathq

    fade voter base cost_bar cost_val cost_lab ben_bar ben_val ben_val4 ben_val2 ben_lab p_read b_read cue_ri to 1

So the voter's ignorance isn't a lapse. It's the right answer to her sum, and everyone in politics can count on it.
    highlight cue_ri

    pause 0.5

## Who shows up
