    icon bakery size 120 set twemoji x 830 y 520 or ["bread","croissant"] icon_look picture of "baguette bread" hidden true
    text fee "$1 fee per\nmainland loaf" x 830 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    text meeting "council meeting:\nMia + her bakers" x 830 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cue_conc "concentrated gain,\nspread-out cost" #b5482e x 830 y 480 font_size 36 hidden true
    text cue_many "× a thousand\nsmall favours" x 830 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Who shows up"

Ignorance is the voter's best deal, and a politician can count on it. Watch what that does in Harbourtown.
    draw axes

B: Say Mia's bakery asks (@draw bakery fee@) the harbour council for a one-dollar fee on every loaf shipped in from the mainland.

With the cheaper mainland bread held back, Mia can charge more. Illust (@draw bar_1@)rative numbers: she gains thirty thousand dollars a year.

B: And each of the town's six thousan (@draw bar_2@)d people pays about six dollars a year more for bread. On this chart, a sliver.

    erase fee

B: So who turns up at the council meeting?
    draw meeting

    pause 0.4

Mia and her bakers. Thirty thousand dollars is worth an evening;  (@highlight bar_2 effect circle@)six dollars isn't worth reading the agenda.
    highlight bar_1

    erase meeting bakery

B: But the council's clerk can multiply. Six thousand peo (@draw bar_3@)ple times six dollars is thirty-six thousand. The town loses more than Mia gains.

    pause 0.3

B: Then surely the council says no. The numbers are right there.
    highlight bar_3

    quiz "How does a councillor who wants to be re-elected vote on the fee?" judge false
        right: Let's see.
        * For the fee
        * Against it

Most likely it says yes. A yes wins Mia's gratitude and her bakers' votes; a no wins nothing, because nobody else noticed.
    highlight bar_1 effect box

    fade bar_3 to 0.3

Concentrated g (@draw cue_conc@)ain, spread-out cost. Nobody was fooled about the numbers: the knowledge was there, and the incentives pointed the other way.

Repeat it over a thousand small fe (@draw cue_many@)es and favours, and a budget can drift a long way from what voters would choose.

    pause 0.5

## Does ignorance cancel out?
sources:
    - id: kff2025
      title: 'KFF Health Tracking Poll February 2025: The Public''s Views on Global Health and USAID'
      authors: KFF
      year: 2025
      finding: 'Average guess: foreign aid is 26% of the federal budget; about 1% is.'
      url: https://www.kff.org/global-health-policy/poll-finding/kff-health-tracking-poll-february-2025-the-publics-views-on-global-health-and-usaid/
    - id: wittman1989
      title: Why Democracies Produce Efficient Results
      authors: Donald Wittman
      year: 1989
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 97(6): 1395–1424. Democratic competition works about as well as economic markets; voter ignorance does not imply bad outcomes.'
    - id: caplan2007
      title: 'The Myth of the Rational Voter: Why Democracies Choose Bad Policies'
      authors: Bryan Caplan
      year: 2007
      finding: Voters' errors about economics are systematic, not random, so they do not cancel out in aggregate.
    - id: mcdonald2024
      title: 2024 General Election Turnout
      authors: University of Florida Election Lab (Michael McDonald)
      year: 2024
      finding: About 64% of the US voting-eligible population voted in 2024.
      url: https://election.lab.ufl.edu/2024-general-election-turnout

