    card title "Does ignorance cancel out?"

B: So democracy is rigged by its own arithmetic? Not everyone buys that.
    draw line lo hi

Donald Wittm (@draw truth truth_lab@)an argued in 1989 that democracies work far better. Ignorant v (@draw cloudA parallel true@)oters guess at random, so (@draw avgA cue_w@) their errors cancel out.

B: And the informed few, plus parties and newspapers handing out cheap shortcuts, tip the result the right way.
    highlight avgA

Bryan Caplan's reply, i (@fade cloudA avgA to 0.25@)n 2007: the errors aren't ra (@draw cloudB parallel true@)ndom. They lean the same way, and leani (@draw avgB cue_c@)ng errors don't cancel.

B: Like the foreign- (@draw cue_aid@)aid guesses. The average missed by twenty-five points. More voters wouldn't fix that average.

    erase cue_aid

B: And there's a doubt about the assumption itself. By that sum, hardly anyone should both (@draw cue_turn@)er to vote. Yet in 2024 nearly two Americans in three did.

So people aren't pure calculators at the ballot box: some feel a duty, and politicians have convictions too.
    highlight cue_turn

    erase cue_turn

How sure are we? That most voters know li (@draw st1@)ttle about policy is mostly agreed. How much th (@draw st2@)at bends policy is contested.

    erase cloudA avgA cue_w cue_c

B: Buchanan's own answer wasn't to give up on government. It w (@draw cue_rules@)as to change its rules, so that the choices that serve voters are also the ones that pay.

    pause 0.3

    erase line lo hi truth truth_lab cloudB avgB cue_rules

    move st1 st2 by [0,250] duration 0.6

    quiz "Say a city plans a $200 million stadium subsidy: about $100 from each of 2 million taxpayers, all of it a gain for one team's owner. Who is more likely to lobby the council?" intro "Four new cases."
        right: The owner: $200 million at stake for one, $100 each for the rest.
        wrong: Compare what each side has at stake per head, not in total: here, the team's owner.
        + The team's owner
        * The taxpayers
        * Both about equally

    quiz "Who is most likely to read the details of a new fishing-quota law?" voice b
        right: The fisher: for her, the law is worth far more than a vote.
        wrong: Knowing pays when the law's effect on you is big, not when your vote might count: here, the fisher.
        + A fisher whose catch depends on it
        * A shopper who buys fish every week
        * A voter in a very close election

    quiz "Voters in a city all overestimate its crime rate, in the same direction. If turnout doubles, does the policy error shrink?"
        right: No: the errors lean one way, so twice the voters means twice the same bias, not a cancelled one.
        wrong: Ask whether the mistakes point both ways or the same way: here, no, the errors lean one way.
        + No: the errors lean one way
        * Yes: more errors cancel out
        * Yes: the informed voters tip it

    quiz "Which reform fits public choice best?" voice b
        right: An expiry rule: it changes what pays, not who governs.
        wrong: Public choice changes the rules people face, not the people: here, the expiry rule.
        + Every subsidy expires unless the council votes for it again
        * Electing kinder councillors
        * Urging voters to study harder

    erase st1 st2

So why do governments fail when politicians follow their incentiv (@draw cue_close@)es? Because looking doesn't pay the many, and lobbying pays the few. And the few have (@draw cue_next@) one more place to go: the regulator.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
