      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1nfzmwm'
    '|b||So democracy is rigged by its own arithmetic? Not everyone buys that.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5840
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Donald Wittman argued in 1989 that democracies work far better. Ignorant voters guess at random, so their errors cancel out.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8040
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:b09vcl'
    '|b||And the informed few, plus parties and newspapers handing out cheap shortcuts, tip the result the right way.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5800
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:pf95db'
    '|a||Bryan Caplan''s reply, in 2007: the errors aren''t random. They lean the same way, and leaning errors don''t cancel.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8920
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:b2n39v'
    '|b||Like the foreign-aid guesses. The average missed by twenty-five points. More voters wouldn''t fix that average.':
